Checked too late!

An Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size vulnerability in Juniper Networks Junos OS SRX 5000 Series devices using SPC2 line cards while ALGs are enabled allows an attacker sending specific crafted packets to cause a transit traffic Denial of Service (DoS).

Continued receipt and processing of these specific packets will sustain the Denial of Service condition.

This issue affects:
Juniper Networks Junos OS SRX 5000 Series with SPC2 with ALGs enabled.
  *  All versions earlier than 21.2R3-S7;
  *  21.4 versions earlier than 21.4R3-S6;
  *  22.1 versions earlier than 22.1R3-S5;
  *  22.2 versions earlier than 22.2R3-S3;
  *  22.3 versions earlier than 22.3R3-S2;
  *  22.4 versions earlier than 22.4R3;
  *  23.2 versions earlier than 23.2R2.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

drm/amd/display: Add 'replay' NULL check in 'edp_set_replay_allow_active()'

In the first if statement, we're checking if 'replay' is NULL. But in
the second if statement, we're not checking if 'replay' is NULL again
before calling replay->funcs->replay_set_power_opt().

if (replay == NULL && force_static)
    return false;

...

if (link->replay_settings.replay_feature_enabled &&
    replay->funcs->replay_set_power_opt) {
	replay->funcs->replay_set_power_opt(replay, *power_opts, panel_inst);
	link->replay_settings.replay_power_opt_active = *power_opts;
}

If 'replay' is NULL, this will cause a null pointer dereference.

Fixes the below found by smatch:
drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/../display/dc/link/protocols/link_edp_panel_control.c:895 edp_set_replay_allow_active() error: we previously assumed 'replay' could be null (see line 887)

A vulnerability in the IPv4 packet processing functionality of Cisco IOS XR Software that affects Cisco ASR 9000 and 9900 Series Aggregation Services Routers as well as Cisco ASR 9902 and 9903 Compact High-Performance Routers could allow an unauthenticated, remote attacker to cause network processor (NP) errors, resulting in a line card reset or shutdown and a denial of service (DoS) condition.&nbsp;

This vulnerability is due to the incorrect handling of crafted IPv4 packets that are received on an affected line card interface and processed as for-us traffic. The issue can result in an NP reset, causing traffic loss on the affected line card while it recovers. Under repeated reset conditions, the network processor may enter a persistent shutdown state, in which case an explicit line card reload may be required to restore traffic.
The vulnerability is within the IPv4 packet-processing path. Transit IPv4 traffic does not trigger this issue and IPv6 traffic cannot trigger it.
